  by: agent  # 3539
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3539
  clock: 2  # 3539
  nonce: '3539'  # 3539
  message: "THE PUBLISHED TEXT IS NOW INVERTED, measured at dev 95041b0931.\n\nSECURITY.md line 35-37 publishes a verdict table:\n\n    inconclusive              0    the analysis could not see enough to confirm\n    confirmed_with_caveats   18    held, but on the strength of an entry this\n                                   repository supplied about itself\n\nand then 18 individual bullets, each opening \"confirmed WITH CAVEATS\".\n\nWhat the tool reports right now: inconclusive 18, confirmed_with_caveats 0.\nExactly inverted, on every line.\n\nTHIS IS THE FAILURE THE GATE WAS CREATED TO PREVENT, by name. Commit\n5c25cb1e4b is titled \"SECURITY.md said the opposite of what the tool reports;\nadd the gate it claimed\", and its rationale records that the published text had\ndrifted until it asserted the OPPOSITE of the tool's output while\nruntime-cli-no-subprocess reported 1101 violating flows. The gate is working:\nit caught the second instance of its own founding defect, at cron cadence,\nwhich is the cadence it was designed for.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR SEQUENCING. SECURITY.md cannot be corrected to \"inconclusive\"\nand left there, and it cannot be left as it is. Either the 13 names are\nadjudicated so the verdicts return to confirmed_with_caveats and the text\nbecomes true again, or the text is rewritten to say the tool currently cannot\nconfirm these claims and the snapshot is re-recorded to match. The first is the\nhonest fix and is what this item is for. The second is a published admission of\nreduced coverage and is the owner's call, not a maintenance action."  # 3539
- op: discuss  # e3fc
  at: '2026-09-14T01:52:16Z'  # e3fc
  by: agent  # e3fc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e3fc
  clock: 3  # e3fc
  nonce: e3fc  # e3fc
  message: "ADJUDICATED 2026-09-13 at dev eb520bc07c. All 19 calls behind the 13 names read\nback against source or the live signature. The count is the point: this is\nauthoring work the mechanism DEMANDS, not a lookup bug.\n\nTHE SHORTCUT IS FORBIDDEN BY DESIGN, and I nearly took it. 12 of the 13 have\ntheir PARENT module already granted (io is granted, io.BytesIO is not), which\nlooks exactly like an exact-vs-prefix matching bug. It is not.\nmodule_io_is_enumerated's own docstring: \"MATCHING IS EXACT. Not a prefix, not\na suffix, not a component.\" An earlier draft DID propagate down a separator and\nwas removed, with the counterexamples recorded: urllib would have vouched for\nurllib.request (opens URLs), and in Go a declaration for os would have vouched\nfor os/exec. The overlay repeats it at its own head: \"Declaring tree_sitter says\nnothing about tree_sitter.Node.\" So every class-qualified receiver needs its own\ndated declaration.\n\nTHIS IS THE THIRD RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE SAME FAILURE. builtins (INV-bofab,\n2026-09-03) and uvicorn, whose overlay note reads \"One entry flipped ALL\nEIGHTEEN of hypergumbo's own claims from confirmed_with_caveats to\ninconclusive\". The mechanism is stable and so is its blast radius.\n\nWHY NOW, and it is not a regression in the usual sense: RECEIVER TYPING GOT\nBETTER. The overlay's coverage row already says \"load()/save() ride the\nuntyped-receiver residual\" - the author knew those calls were uncovered and\nrecorded them as an accepted residual, because an untyped receiver is disclosed\nthrough the untyped_receiver caveat rather than counted as blindness. Phase 6\ntyping made those receivers resolvable, so a DISCLOSED residual became a\nBLOCKING unknown. An improvement in one place invalidated evidence in another.\n\nTHE ADJUDICATION. Split by where the declaration belongs, which decides the\nblast radius: stdlib names go in the shipped python.yaml and move every Python\nrepo; third-party names go in this repo's own overlay and move only hypergumbo.\n\n  SHIPPED python.yaml (stdlib)\n    io.BytesIO            getvalue            in-memory buffer. Declare.\n    io.StringIO           getvalue            in-memory buffer. Declare.\n    random.Random         choice, randint     PRNG over in-memory state. Declare.\n    concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor\n                          submit              schedules a callable; the callable\n                                              owns its own I/O edges. Declare.\n    urllib.request.Request\n                          add_header          constructs and sends nothing -\n                                              already adjudicated by INV-gujoh\n                                              and WI-gihif. Declare.\n    zipfile.ZipFile       extractall          REAL fs_write. ROW REQUIRED.\n                          write               REAL fs_read - \"Put the bytes from\n                                              filename into the archive\" reads\n                                              filename off disk even when the\n                                              archive is an in-memory buffer.\n                                              ROW REQUIRED.\n    subprocess.Popen      communicate         see BLOCKER 1.\n    typing.TextIO         write               see BLOCKER 2.\n\n  OVERLAY hypergumbo-self.yaml (third-party, repo-scoped)\n    coverage.Coverage     load                reads the .coverage DB off disk.\n                                              ROW REQUIRED (fs_read).\n                          analysis2           reads the measured SOURCE file.\n                                              ROW REQUIRED (fs_read).\n                          get_data            in-memory accessor after load.\n    coverage.parser.PythonParser\n                          parse_source        constructed as\n                                              PythonParser(filename=path) at\n                                              coverage_census.py:342, so\n                                              parse_source READS THAT FILE.\n                                              ROW REQUIRED (fs_read).\n                          first_line          computation over parsed state.\n    ruamel.yaml.YAML      dump                ry.dump([prepared], stream) at\n                                              store.py:335 - writes to a stream\n                                              the CALLER opened, the json/tomli\n                                              shape this overlay already cites.\n                                              Declare, no row.\n    rich.table.Table      add_column, add_row builds a renderable; rich.console\n                                              is where writing happens and is\n                                              already rowed. Declare.\n    tree_sitter.Parser    parse               source already in memory, the same\n                                              rationale as the existing\n                                              tree_sitter entry. Declare.\n\nA REAL FINDING INSIDE THE BOOKKEEPING: zf.extractall(tmppath) at\ngitleaks.py:231 is an unarchive-to-disk carrying a bandit S202 suppression, and\nit sits inside the very subcommand install-gitleaks-no-host-fs makes a claim\nabout. The catalogue cannot currently see it. The gate going inconclusive is\nPROTECTIVE here, not noise.\n\nBLOCKER 1 - subprocess.Popen.communicate. NOT the threat-model question I first\nthought, and WI-bimik's premise is stale (recorded separately on that item):\nsubprocess output already mints untrusted_input at tip via the ipc_recv row for\ncheck_output/getoutput/getstatusoutput, simultaneous: true, per INV-lozat and\nADR-0049 ruling 1. communicate belongs with run, not with check_output: whether\nit transfers depends on stdout=PIPE, an argument the catalogue cannot read, and\nhere that argument is on the CONSTRUCTOR - one hop further away than run's own\ncapture_output=. The shipped treatment for exactly that is a launch-only row\nwith the omission disclosed rather than silent. So this is resolvable on\nprecedent, and is NOT a blocker after all.\n\nBLOCKER 2 - typing.TextIO.write, AND THIS ONE IS REAL. The receiver is typed\nfrom an annotation (stdout: TextIO = sys.stdout, test_masking.py:323). A\nTextIO may be a file, a pipe or an in-memory buffer, so no single boundary is\ntrue: fs_write for one binding, ipc_send or logging for another. Declaring\ntyping.TextIO complete would assert it does no I/O, which is false. Only\ncompleteness: \"complete\" vouches - there is no partial or declared-blind value\nfor a MODULE (CONSTRUCT_BLIND_ROWS declares ROWS unreachable, a different\nthing). This is the receiver-origin io_target_kind family: WI-tusav names it\nfor java (BufferedReader.readLine as a FIXED fs_read row, so a stdin read mints\nnothing) and WI-bapuk for c/cpp (the stream is an ARGUMENT, the fgets shape).\nThat mechanism is DESIGNED AND UNBUILT.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, stated plainly: qualifying_only is \"not unknown\", so the set must\nbe EMPTY. Twelve of thirteen are resolvable now; the thirteenth needs an\nunbuilt mechanism. Authoring the twelve therefore restores NOTHING on its own\nand the gate stays red until typing.TextIO has an honest home. That is not an\nargument for skipping the twelve - the zipfile and coverage rows are genuine\nmissing coverage regardless - but it must not be sold as fixing the gate."  # e3fc
